^^Adessive, allative, ablative^^

In the last skill we learned the *internal locative cases*, now it's time for the *external locative cases*. Same principle as previously, only the thing is "outside of", "on", or comes "from on top of" something.

The suffixes we use are:

on = *-lla/-llä* "kirjalla" (on the book), *pöydällä* (on the table)
onto = *-lle* "kirjalle" (onto the book), *pöydälle* (onto the table)
from on top of = *-lta/-ltä* "kirjalta" (from on top of the book), *pöydältä* (from on top of the table)

It might seem stupid to translate the last one to English as "from on top of the table" when we just as easily could say "from the table", but in this case we want to *really show you* how the *-lta/-ltä* -form differs from the *-sta/-stä* -form.

As before, the third one (*ablative*) is used a lot more often than the others.

^Examples of ablative^

Everything that gives you a sensation (hearing, smell etc.) is in ablative:

"se tuntuu hyvä*ltä*" (it feels good)
"Kuulostaa hyvä*ltä*!" (That sounds good!)
"ruoka maistuu paha*lta*" (the food tastes bad)
"sinä haiset roskikse*lta*" (you smell like a garbage can)
"näyttää huono*lta*" (it's looking bad), "hän näyttää presidentiltä" (she looks like the president)

Note: don't use "se" (it) in a sentence like "it's looking bad". *Se näyttää huonolta* means some concrete thing is looking really unwell, while *näyttää huonolta* "without *se*" means the situation itself is looking bad.

Note 2: just so you know, in spoken Finnish you might hear "ruoka maistuu paha*lle*" instead of "paha*lta*", but this comes from dialect and is grammatically incorrect.